WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:10.600
Does twice the voltage always mean twice the current? Here is a resistor of ten ohms with two volts across it. Now we make it four volts.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.120
Pause and predict the current at four volts. Then decide whether always is safe.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:32.420
At two volts, the current is zero point two amperes. At four volts, it is zero point four amperes. Twice the voltage, twice the current.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:45.560
For this resistor at constant temperature, voltage divided by current stays ten ohms at both points. That constant is the resistance: voltage equals current multiplied by resistance.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:56.600
But always is not safe. A filament bulb gets hot, so its resistance rises, and the current no longer doubles. The rule needs constant resistance.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:05.320
So, for a conductor at constant temperature, current is proportional to voltage, and resistance is the constant ratio.

